Now I see the allocation for scanline images:

```c
cunpsz *= w / xsamp;       // floor(w / xsamp)
cunpsz *= linePerChunk;    // full chunk height
if (ysamp > 1)
{
    hasLineSample = 1;
    if (linePerChunk > 1) cunpsz *= linePerChunk / ysamp;  // floor(linePerChunk / ysamp)
}
```

So `unpacked_size_per_chunk` is `bpc * floor(w/xsamp) * linePerChunk * floor(linePerChunk/ysamp)` for channels with y_sampling > 1.

But in `compute_chunk_unpack_size`, for the last chunk with height < linePerChunk:
```c
chansz = bpc * width / x_sampling;  // floor(width / x_sampling)
chansz *= height;                    // actual chunk height
if (y_sampling > 1)
{
    if (height > 1)
    {
        if (y_sampling > height)
            chansz /= height;
        else
            chansz /= y_sampling;
    }
    ...
}
```

So for the last chunk with `height = 5` and `y_sampling = 3`:
- `chansz = bpc * width / x_sampling * 5 / 3 = bpc * width / x_sampling * 1` (integer division: 5/3 = 1)

But `generic_unpack` reads `ceil(5/3) * floor(width/x_sampling) * bpc = 2 * floor(width/x_sampling) * bpc` bytes.

So the allocation is `1 * floor(width/x_sampling) * bpc` but the read is `2 * floor(width/x_sampling) * bpc`. The read is 2x the allocation.

